And there's a bunch more.

18:04.880 --> 18:07.360
But the general gist of it is that you just

18:07.360 --> 18:09.600
have this bunch of functions you need to implement,

18:09.600 --> 18:13.800
and then the framework knows how to call you correctly.

18:13.800 --> 18:19.640
That's really all you need for the pesky stuff. Once again, the documents are really great to

18:19.640 --> 18:24.520
look into how this works in specifics. Also, of course, just look at existing platforms.

18:24.520 --> 18:28.440
My favorite is always the Rockchip one because the SSCs tend to be really simple. They don't have

18:28.440 --> 18:34.280
any of the weird hooks and crannies that some of the other more complicated SSCs have, so they

18:34.280 --> 18:39.880
usually make a good example. Once you have that, you're essentially done supporting Corewood,

18:39.880 --> 18:43.560
but you can do some more stuff if you want to. One of the common things you might want to do

18:43.560 --> 18:49.320
is custom smcs of course this pesky also uses smcs and all of those are already implemented

18:49.320 --> 18:52.600
in the framework so you don't need to worry about those but you can add your own if you want to

18:53.880 --> 18:59.640
common thing that we need find that we need is one time ddr frequency scaling for example because

18:59.640 --> 19:04.360
if you do anything that involves memory since memory is used by the whole platform usually

19:04.360 --> 19:09.080
it needs to go through the highest privilege level to allow that and it's also pretty simple

19:09.640 --> 19:13.960
they essentially have a macro that you can use to define a handler for a given range of smc

19:13.960 --> 19:19.400
function ids there's actually this whole big specification from arm that tells you which

19:19.400 --> 19:25.080
smc function id ranges are allowed to be used by whom usually the one you want to use is called

19:25.080 --> 19:29.960
the sip range i always forget what it stands for but it's essentially the soc vendor range so if

19:29.960 --> 19:36.360
you're implementing an soc port that's usually the one you want to use and last but not least

19:36.360 --> 19:43.240
we have some fun core boot support in there now um one of the problems if with integrating

19:43.240 --> 19:48.840
core wood is that there's not yet a great way to pass parameters in a generic way from core boot

19:48.840 --> 19:53.160
into trusted firmware this is something that i think the trusted firmware team is interested in

19:53.160 --> 20:00.280
making better in the future but right now all that they have is uh essentially you can have one

20:00.280 --> 20:05.080
pointer of platform specific information and then the platform has to figure out how to deal with

20:05.080 --> 20:11.640
that so when you the first piece of platform code that's called when you are in trusted firmware is

20:11.640 --> 20:16.600
usually the function over there which gets that one pointer and then you have to figure out what

20:16.600 --> 20:21.560
to do with that usually we find that we have to pass a bunch of things from core boot into trusted

20:21.560 --> 20:26.040
firmware so what we do is we build a sort of primitive linked list with that and then parse it

20:27.160 --> 20:33.080
when like at the very first thing in trusted firmware um and one of the things you should

20:33.080 --> 20:37.720
passes the core boot table base address because then you can use this core boot table setup

20:37.720 --> 20:42.600
function which we have in trusted firmware which gives you all the core boot the generic core boot

20:42.600 --> 20:47.880
support that we implemented which is right now mostly console support so for example after that

20:47.880 --> 20:54.600
you have access to the console the uart information over there so you don't have to hard code the uart

20:54.600 --> 20:59.720
base address anymore you can use the one from core boot and more importantly you essentially

20:59.720 --> 21:03.720
take over coreboots configuration with that so if you disable the uart and coreboot kconfig then

21:03.720 --> 21:07.640
it would just not pass that and then trusted firmware will also not use it which is nicer than

21:07.640 --> 21:12.920
having to disable stuff in every uh every different repository your firmware is made of

21:13.560 --> 21:17.720
um the other nice thing you get with this automatically is cbmem console support so as

21:17.720 --> 21:22.600
soon as you like if you have a cbmem console in coreboot then information about that is passed

21:22.600 --> 21:27.160
in the coreboot table and then trusted firmware automatically register console driver for that

21:27.160 --> 21:32.120
and then once you boot into user land, you can dump the CBMIM console and see all the

21:32.120 --> 21:38.840
trusted firmware logs right after the core boot logs. I think this is pretty much all

21:38.840 --> 21:44.360
I want to talk about about the basic trusted firmware architecture. The one thing I want

21:44.360 --> 21:51.320
to leave you with is a little anecdote about some of the practical problems we had when implementing

21:51.320 --> 21:57.960
one of these socs and trusted firmware this is about the rock chip 3399 and um well we essentially

21:57.960 --> 22:03.800
had some trouble fitting everything there um this story starts very simple with bringing up the chip

22:03.800 --> 22:09.400
and trying to get suspend resume support working so of course the whole system suspend resume also

22:09.400 --> 22:13.400
needs to go through the secure monitor because it turns off the whole system so the highest

22:13.400 --> 22:19.400
privileged never needs to know about it and um you usually turn out like the ddr controller usually

22:19.400 --> 22:22.840
uses a state during that, so you have to reinitialize it somehow when you come back up.

22:22.840 --> 22:27.880
And, of course, the code for that can't be in DRAM. So thankfully, we are an ARM SOC. We

22:27.880 --> 22:35.080
usually have a bunch of SRAMs, so we can put it in there. And we have quite a lot of SRAM

22:35.080 --> 22:40.360
on this platform, so that's pretty easy to fit. Then we had some other issues about

22:41.960 --> 22:47.240
we want to do some DDR frequency scaling at runtime. Depending on the SOC, this is sometimes

22:47.240 --> 22:51.720
pretty easy and sometimes a real chore on this one it was thankfully not that hard because we

22:51.720 --> 22:57.640
have this little separate microcontroller in the soc like a cortex m0 essentially on the side of

22:57.640 --> 23:03.960
the normal cpus which had the ability to just stop the cpus in place retrain ddr to a different

23:03.960 --> 23:08.760
frequency and then kick them off again the main cpus never really knew what did them so this was

23:08.760 --> 23:14.200
pretty easy to implement but we still needed uh to put some code in like we needed to host that

23:14.200 --> 23:18.920
code for this M0 in SRAM, of course, because DDR is inaccessible while we're changing frequencies.

23:20.680 --> 23:25.240
And we had some more fun with trying to work around hardware bugs. So this is a particular

23:25.240 --> 23:30.840
thing where when it goes into system suspend and it gets a wake interrupt at just the right time,

23:30.840 --> 23:35.640
like just after you turned off the main CPU, then sometimes it wouldn't wake up again.

23:35.640 --> 23:40.040
So to work around that, you essentially needed something to sit around and wait until after all

23:40.040 --> 23:42.940
All the CPUs have been turned off and checked

23:42.940 --> 23:44.700
for this condition and then cleared.

23:44.700 --> 23:46.740
So here comes our M0 to the rescue again,

23:46.740 --> 23:48.860
and we had to implement some code for that.

23:48.860 --> 23:51.440
All of this still fit pretty nicely into SRAM.

23:51.440 --> 23:56.260
But then we had a great idea on how to save some more power.

23:56.260 --> 23:59.340
This is where I wanted to be, which is essentially

23:59.340 --> 24:01.700
there was one more power rail we could turn off,

24:01.700 --> 24:03.920
which powered most of the chip.

24:03.920 --> 24:07.140
And turning it off would have saved us really a bunch of power.

24:07.140 --> 24:09.400
Unfortunately, it also powered the SRAM.

24:09.400 --> 24:12.360
So, at that point, we sort of have a problem,

24:12.360 --> 24:13.500
because we can turn the system off,

24:13.500 --> 24:14.580
but when we turn it back on,

24:14.580 --> 24:17.260
we don't have anything to resume from.

24:17.260 --> 24:19.220
So, the one good thing we had was

24:19.220 --> 24:21.980
we have this little amount of PMU SRAM,

24:21.980 --> 24:25.100
which is sort of a separate amount of SRAM

24:25.100 --> 24:28.020
that's implemented, that's powered by a different power rail,

24:28.020 --> 24:30.460
and would stay alive even if we turn this off.

24:30.460 --> 24:32.640
But now we have to start fitting all of this stuff in there,

24:32.640 --> 24:34.360
which is kind of awful.

24:35.360 --> 24:37.480
The DDR in it stuff, so it kind of fits.

24:38.360 --> 24:42.680
one of the problems that we also have here is that the entry point of the cpu when it comes

24:42.680 --> 24:48.120
back from resume you can essentially only give the soc the top 60 bits so it has to be 64k aligned

24:48.120 --> 24:51.880
thankfully the pm usrm is 64k aligned but it needs to sit at the very top for that

24:52.600 --> 24:57.000
and the m0 code also needs to be 4k aligned because that's how the m0 works so

24:57.720 --> 25:01.560
first of all putting the whole m0 code that we have right now in there doesn't fit at all there's

25:01.560 --> 25:08.120
no way. Our idea was rather than having one M0 image and giving it an argument of which

25:08.120 --> 25:12.440
of the two functions it's supposed to do, we could split it up and have two M0 images

25:12.440 --> 25:17.320
and then just always give the M0 base address for the one we want to use when we kick it

25:17.320 --> 25:21.320
off. This sort of works, but if you can see between

25:21.320 --> 25:26.080
these two, it overlaps part of the DDR and its stuff, so it doesn't quite fit, and it

25:26.080 --> 25:29.240
needs to be in the middle there because it needs to be 4K aligned, so this also didn't

25:29.240 --> 25:36.500
quite work. But then we realized the DDR on it has to start at 64k. But after that, it's

25:36.500 --> 25:41.260
really just normal code. So we can really sort of rip it apart and sandwich it carefully

25:41.260 --> 25:51.760
around the M0 code. And come on. With a lot of, you know, juggling bits and counting instructions,

25:51.760 --> 25:57.800
we managed to get this all in 8k. You have one problem left if you do all this, which

25:57.800 --> 26:01.320
which is that since you lose SRAM in S3,

26:01.320 --> 26:03.760
of course, you lose that DDR frequency scanning stuff,

26:03.760 --> 26:06.400
so it would be not that great

26:06.400 --> 26:09.240
if DDR frequency scanning worked after you boot it,

26:09.240 --> 26:11.000
but then once you suspend resume once,

26:11.000 --> 26:12.360
it doesn't work anymore.

26:12.360 --> 26:16.120
So the ugly but obvious solution is to cache it in DRAM.

26:18.240 --> 26:20.520
I think this thing is dying there.

26:22.040 --> 26:26.360
And then just copy it back every time we resume, essentially.

26:26.360 --> 26:28.100
So if you want to look how this madness

26:28.100 --> 26:32.980
looks in the link to script, I've got it over here.

26:32.980 --> 26:35.100
Can we switch the slides manually?

26:35.100 --> 26:37.520
Thanks.

26:37.520 --> 26:40.600
So the ink bin stuff is essentially the M0 code.

26:40.600 --> 26:43.060
So you see it needs to be 64K aligned.

26:43.060 --> 26:46.920
And then even though the other stuff is the same program,

26:46.920 --> 26:48.680
if we put the data section beyond that,

26:48.680 --> 26:50.580
it just happens to magically work out.

26:50.580 --> 26:52.700
And we're just hoping that we never

26:52.700 --> 26:54.100
have a compiler change or anything

26:54.100 --> 26:55.280
that messes all of this up again,

26:55.280 --> 26:57.040
because it's going to be the most awful thing.

26:59.820 --> 27:03.680
If you want to see some details about how

27:03.680 --> 27:05.180
you do this sort of thing where you

27:05.180 --> 27:07.640
build the binary for a completely different chip

27:07.640 --> 27:10.220
inside the same software as the main,

27:10.220 --> 27:12.060
inside your main software package,

27:12.060 --> 27:14.760
and link it all together, it's somehow done like this.

27:14.760 --> 27:16.360
First of all, you essentially first

27:16.360 --> 27:20.300
build that M0 code completely separate into a strip binary.

27:20.300 --> 27:22.200
And then you would define a makefile rule

27:22.200 --> 27:24.520
for the main software, where that binary

27:24.520 --> 27:28.600
is another dependency for one of the C files.

27:28.600 --> 27:30.340
And you also pass the name of that binary

27:30.340 --> 27:34.140
in as a preprocessor macro.

27:34.140 --> 27:36.780
And then you can use this weird assembly instruction

27:36.780 --> 27:39.120
called inkbin, which essentially just tells the assembler

27:39.120 --> 27:42.280
to, at this point, include a whole other file completely

27:42.280 --> 27:44.140
and then continue assembling.

27:44.140 --> 27:45.640
And then you put a label around of it,

27:45.640 --> 27:47.800
and then you can access it from C code

27:47.800 --> 27:52.600
and tell the M0-based address, which is to put the address

27:52.600 --> 27:54.440
in there and then sort of work out.

27:54.440 --> 27:58.160
And this is the M0 code we run on suspend in the end.

27:58.160 --> 28:00.500
As you can see, it's really simple and really stupid.

28:00.500 --> 28:03.780
There's essentially this suspend state,

28:03.780 --> 28:07.340
which keeps counting upwards as you go through the suspend

28:07.340 --> 28:09.480
and then again as you go through the resume later.

28:09.480 --> 28:11.180
As you can see, we had some other issues we also

28:11.180 --> 28:12.740
had to work around in the end.

28:12.740 --> 28:14.840
Like as you keep testing more and more on the platform,

28:14.840 --> 28:16.700
you keep finding more things.

28:16.700 --> 28:19.360
This is actually where the system is suspended in here.

28:19.360 --> 28:22.160
So if you take one of these Chromebooks today

28:22.160 --> 28:25.540
and you close the lid, then even though it's supposed

28:25.540 --> 28:27.880
to save power, the whole time where the lid is closed,

28:27.880 --> 28:31.060
you have this little codex M0 saying in a tight loop,

28:31.060 --> 28:32.160
am I already resumed?

28:32.160 --> 28:33.160
Am I already resumed?

28:33.160 --> 28:34.700
Am I already resumed?

28:34.700 --> 28:36.840
But it turns out that M0s don't eat a lot of power.

28:36.840 --> 28:38.980
And if you do that on a 32 kilohertz clock,

28:38.980 --> 28:41.080
it's actually not as crazy as it sounds.

28:41.080 --> 28:45.940
You just shouldn't know how the sausage is made in that case.

28:45.940 --> 28:46.900
Yeah, that's all I have.

