29:07.560 --> 29:14.560
Why does the DDR3 controller need to be re-initialized after S3 resume?

29:14.560 --> 29:22.560
Why, for instance, can the hardware put that in self-refresh and that boot, it works basically?

29:22.560 --> 29:28.960
Right, so this is not the DDR itself, right, so the DDR chip on the board is put into self-refresh,

29:28.960 --> 29:35.520
but the DDR controller on the SOC needs to know how to talk to the DDR, and that part isn't

29:35.520 --> 29:40.640
maintained mostly to save power, so we shut off most parts of the SOC when we go into S3 and that's

29:40.640 --> 29:42.020
That's one of the parts we shut off.

29:42.020 --> 29:44.720
So after the boot, you need something

29:44.720 --> 29:47.700
to tell the DDR controller how the DDR chip,

29:47.700 --> 29:50.960
like how many rows, banks, and columns, and so on,

29:50.960 --> 29:53.360
you have, and to tell it to take the DDR out

29:53.360 --> 29:55.080
of self-refresh, and all that kind of code

29:55.080 --> 29:56.820
needs to live somewhere.

29:56.820 --> 30:00.040
And second question.

30:00.040 --> 30:05.000
Why don't you do the initialization part

30:05.000 --> 30:10.100
like a normal boot, like in core boots on x86?

30:10.100 --> 30:12.980
Are you asking why we're not reading it from Flash?

30:12.980 --> 30:16.940
Yes, why aren't you doing it dynamically

30:16.940 --> 30:18.920
from code that is in Flash?

30:18.920 --> 30:22.460
Not necessarily saving that on Flash, but.

30:22.460 --> 30:23.800
You can do that.

30:23.800 --> 30:25.460
It sort of depends on the SoC.

30:25.460 --> 30:27.860
So this SoC in particular, I think,

30:27.860 --> 30:30.000
was designed to resume from SRAM.

30:30.000 --> 30:32.600
So I'm not sure if starting the boot

30:32.600 --> 30:34.940
ROM again from Flash would even be possible.

30:34.940 --> 30:36.720
The other reason you might not want that

30:36.720 --> 30:40.000
is it sort of depends on how your system is designed.

30:40.000 --> 30:42.340
In our case, this is a Chromebook.

30:42.340 --> 30:44.080
So Chromebooks are always designed in a way

30:44.080 --> 30:46.020
to boot from a read-only partition

30:46.020 --> 30:49.380
and then do some crypto magic before we jump into the partition

30:49.380 --> 30:51.760
we can actually update on the flash.

30:51.760 --> 30:56.440
And we found out in the past that tying the resume path

30:56.440 --> 30:59.000
to this read-only flash is a very bad idea,

30:59.000 --> 31:02.760
because that means that if you ever have a problem in your resume

31:02.760 --> 31:05.320
path, in your early resume path, you can't really fix it.

31:05.320 --> 31:07.480
So resuming from SOM is very nice,

31:07.480 --> 31:09.020
because then you can always update it.

31:09.020 --> 31:13.240
And you're not bound to the stuff you have put on Flash, essentially.

