and then passing on to the next part.

36:49.260 --> 36:52.200
And it's really only meant to do these runtime services.

36:52.200 --> 36:55.620
So it's usually below 15 milliseconds

36:55.620 --> 36:59.800
to go through the processor for my part.

36:59.800 --> 37:01.800
Yep, just out of curiosity, I think

37:01.800 --> 37:03.300
on that Rackchip platform, there was

37:03.300 --> 37:07.860
a dedicated microcontroller for the DRAM suspend stuff.

37:07.860 --> 37:09.880
The DCF, can you tell us the story about that

37:09.880 --> 37:12.440
and why it was replaced by the M0?

37:12.440 --> 37:16.900
Yeah, I don't think I can remember all the details.

37:16.900 --> 37:20.360
The DCF was this thing that they implemented specifically

37:20.360 --> 37:22.480
to do the frequency switching.

37:22.480 --> 37:25.000
And it was like their own little microcontroller

37:25.000 --> 37:26.380
with only a couple of instructions

37:26.380 --> 37:28.900
and really crazy thing, essentially.

37:28.900 --> 37:30.520
We didn't have an assembler for that.

37:30.520 --> 37:32.120
We had to ask them for code for it.

37:32.120 --> 37:34.640
And I don't really remember the details.

37:34.640 --> 37:35.980
But at some point, essentially, we

37:35.980 --> 37:37.640
found something that we needed to do

37:37.640 --> 37:39.400
during the frequency scaling that we couldn't

37:39.400 --> 37:40.520
with the DCF.

37:40.520 --> 37:42.160
And then we thought, why are we doing this

37:42.160 --> 37:44.300
if we just have a full ARM core there that

37:44.300 --> 37:46.840
can do everything, and it's fully general purpose.

37:46.840 --> 37:48.100
So we switched to using that.

37:55.240 --> 37:58.280
Oh, is there room for another two questions, maybe?

38:09.400 --> 38:16.440
In terms of whether to use the, like, you know, you pulled the one part out, and then

38:16.440 --> 38:18.700
the question was, you know, what about those earlier parts?

38:18.700 --> 38:23.580
Do you know their policy on, you know, booting versus bricking?

38:23.700 --> 38:26.840
In other words, the core boot thing is it'll always come up, and it'll just tell you things

38:26.840 --> 38:29.240
didn't go well, but do you know what the ARM stuff does?

38:29.380 --> 38:30.460
In that case, I'm just curious.

38:32.080 --> 38:34.540
I'm not really familiar, honestly, with those other two parts.

38:34.880 --> 38:35.100
Okay.

38:35.100 --> 38:39.760
But the thing is that I think it's a very thin framework,

38:39.960 --> 38:41.780
so a lot of the stuff is left to the platform,

38:41.900 --> 38:44.560
and the platform is supposed to figure those kinds of things out,

38:44.620 --> 38:46.100
like what happens if it fails or something.

38:46.500 --> 38:50.280
I think the actual generic framework parts for BL1 and BL2

38:50.280 --> 38:53.360
are very lightweight and don't really do that much.

38:53.360 --> 39:07.240
All right, if there are no more questions,

39:07.240 --> 39:09.420
then thanks again very much, Julius.

